  "body": "Steps like\r\n\r\n> When a Document in an iframe is marked as completely loaded, the user agent must run the iframe load event steps.\r\n\r\nare not great. Here's an audit of all \"completely loaded\" usages and how we could make them better.\r\n\r\n### Setting \"completely loaded\"\r\n\r\n- [A monkeypatch to createDocument() or createHTMLDocument()](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#documents:completely-loaded). See below.\r\n- [Creating the initial about:blank](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#creating-browsing-contexts:completely-loaded)\r\n- [At the end of parsing](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#completely-loaded) (also serves as the definition, currently)\r\n\r\nCases that never get it set:\r\n\r\n- XHR responseDocument\r\n- DOMParser parseFromString() Documents\r\n- temporary Documents used in fragment parsing\r\n- template content owner documents\r\n\r\nSee below for some thoughts on those.\r\n\r\n### Reacting to \"completely loaded\"\r\n\r\nThese should be turned into direct algorithm calls instead\r\n\r\n- [Document in an iframe](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-iframe-element:completely-loaded-2)\r\n- [Document in an embed](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-embed-element:completely-loaded)\r\n- [Document in a frame](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#frames:completely-loaded-2)\r\n\r\nNote that a `Document` in an `object` is missing. Edit: it's not missing; it just uses a non-linked version of \"completely loaded\" that also, sorta, applies to plugins.\r\n\r\n### Consulting \"completely loaded\"\r\n\r\nAll other cases are checking \"completely loaded\" as a boolean. This is fine.\r\n\r\nWhat's interesting here is that all of these checks are guarded by checking for documents with a browsing context. (Except `<meta http=equiv=\"refresh\">`, but that seems to be a larger problem where none of the `<meta http-equiv>`s should work.)\r\n\r\nThis means that it doesn't matter what we do for the \"cases that never get it set\", and also it doesn't matter what we do for `createDocument()` and `createHTMLDocument()`.\r\n\r\nSo, I think we can simplify things a good bit.",
